    "final_text": "I've built the CSV library: `src/csv.js` (CommonJS) exports `parse`, `createParser`, `stringify` and `CsvError`, with no dependencies. The full test suite (196 tests) passes through the Dex gate, and a syntax check of all files is clean.\n\n**Layout.** `src/csv.js` is the entry point. The real code is in `src/parser.js`, `src/stringify.js`, `src/options.js` and `src/errors.js`. `parse` just feeds the whole string to the streaming parser in one piece, so chunked input can't give a different result or error position than whole input. Each character is read once, and a field that spans chunks is joined only when it ends, so time and memory grow with the input size.\n\n**Tests.** There are five files under `test/`, one each for `parse`, `stringify`, error positions, streaming and round trips. `npm test` runs them (`node --test`).\n- **Chunk splits:** 22 inputs, valid and invalid, are fed through every 2-way and 3-way split plus one character at a time, and each result must match `parse` exactly.\n- **Round trips:** random tables are checked under 7 delimiters, both line endings, and with and without `skipEmptyLines`.\n- **Speed:** a stress test feeds over 300,000 one-character chunks; it takes about 28 ms.\n- **Do the tests catch bugs?** I broke the code on purpose in eight places (for example, splitting a CRLF across chunks, or dropping the end of a field at a chunk boundary). The suite caught every one.\n\n**Fixed during testing:**\n- **Wrong error position:** a duplicate header name that ends at end of input (header `,` or `k,,`) was reported at the first field's position. It's fixed, with a regression test.\n- **Invisible characters:** the file-writing tool had saved my `\\uFEFF` and similar escapes in the tests as literal invisible characters. I changed them back to escapes.\n\n**Choices where the spec was open**, all listed in the README:\n- **BOM:** a leading byte order mark doesn't count as a column, so the character after it is column 1.\n- **Delimiter:** \"one character\" means `delimiter.length === 1`, so an emoji delimiter is rejected with `TypeError`.\n- **Duplicate headers:** a duplicate is reported as soon as the repeated field ends. So for `a,a,\"x` you get the duplicate, not the unclosed quote.\n- **Header with no rows:** `stringify([], { header: [...] })` writes the header line instead of returning `''`.\n- **BOM in output:** if the output would start with U+FEFF, `stringify` quotes that first field. Otherwise `parse` would strip it as a byte order mark and the round trip would lose it.\n- **Header objects:** a column named `__proto__` becomes a normal property. When writing, only an object's own properties are read, so a column named `toString` comes out empty rather than picking up a built-in.\n- **Shared options:** each function ignores options only the other one uses, so one options object works for both directions.\n\nThe work is in five commits on the `feat/csv-parser` branch. There's no remote, so nothing was pushed. A project hook required conventional-commit messages and a Dex co-author line, so the commits use those instead of the Claude attribution. I left `CLAUDE.md` untracked, as it was when I started.",
